A||||5||GodSend||Simple Timing Chain Question..||||1||
Z||000000||GodSend||08-18-2000||12:32 PM||||  Please forgive my newbee question, but Is there any damage to the engine when a timing chain goes in a Fiero? (My talon would receive substantial damaage when the belt breaks).  <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||08-18-2000||01:31 PM||||From what I understand no Fiero engine will be damaged if a timing chain(v-6) or timing gear(I-4) let go. Engines like your Talon had are called "impact engines" since if the chain or gear break, the valves will impact the pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-18-2000||01:33 PM||||our 6s if they toss a chain will not have clearance problems... they are not an interferiance engine. I'm not sure about the 4s. many american cars were built with this in mind while the Japanese, Italian and korean makes were built to CRAZY close specs and will like your Talon waste a head, a piston and who knows what else... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||08-18-2000||04:03 PM||||Having lost teeth off of my camshaft gear in my 4 cylinder not once, but 4 times  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, I can tell you that no other damage will occur in the Duke.  After the last time I bought a set of Super Duty gears.  They are all metal and should last a lot longer than the phenolic camshaft gear.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||4
Z||000004||terryk||08-18-2000||04:47 PM||||I wish my V6 had known this when the timing chain broke and bent 4 valves. I'll remind it next time.<P>TK||||reg||5
Z||000005||EdsB52||08-18-2000||07:24 PM||||I believe both the 2.5 and 2.8 do not have valve clearance pistons. I respectfully disagree with a previous poster who claimed that Jap, Italian, and other foriegn cars are not of the valve clearance design, while American cars are. I know for a fact that all, or most of the old Supras are valve clearance. How many American cars are hemi's anymore? How many American cars are all aluminum or at least have aluminum heads? I can wave a flag like the next guy, but let's face it, Japanese auto makers strive for the best product, whereas American auto makers strive for planned obsolesence. Why was the Fiero killed? Was it because it was going whip ass on the Corvette as discussed in this forum? Just as GM started perfecting it, it was quashed. I hold no praise for American auto makers, and they are not a reflection or representation of America, just of corporate America.<P>Back to the hemi issue. It's obviously / arguably the most efficient combustion chamber available. So why have the American auto makers gone away from it? My guess is cost. They require us to pay fifty f'n grand for a car that hauls ass anymore. Look at anything Japanese. They will almost uncertainly utilize the hemispherical combustion chamber in most applications because they can get the most from it. That's one of the reasons a 600cc Jap bike will smoke any 1340 Harley. There's no replacement for displacement!!! Whatever!<P>Back to the issue. Just because you throw a chain doesn't mean you will bend valves. The higher the RPM, the more likely. I worked on an old AMC with a teflon timing gear (not a misprint). They're good for about 70,000 miles. Anyway, the valves weren't bent, but the pushrods were. It's crap-shoot.||||reg||1
